<description>
Fill whole lattice cells along straight red and green marker runs, coloring red/green intersections pink.

Input structure
- A teal lattice divides the canvas into rectangular background cells.
- Red and green marker pixels sit inside some cells of that lattice.
- Markers of the same color are paired by sharing a lattice row or a lattice column.

Rule
- For each color, any lattice row with at least two same-color markers is filled between the leftmost and rightmost marker cells.
- Likewise, any lattice column with at least two same-color markers is filled between the topmost and bottommost marker cells.
- Fill entire cells, preserve the teal lattice, and color cells reached by both red and green runs pink.

Key insight
- The marker pixels are not expanded locally; they are endpoints in a coarser grid whose rows and columns are the lattice cells.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "red and green marker pixels inside cells of the teal lattice",
  "path": "straight horizontal or vertical lattice-cell run from a marker cell to a same-color marker cell sharing its lattice row or column",
  "dst": "the aligned same-color marker cell at the other end of the run",
  "trajectory": "fill every whole lattice cell on the inclusive run with the marker color",
  "transform": "preserve the teal lattice; cells covered by both red and green runs become pink"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 22-28 square, same size for rows and cols
  lattice:
    color: teal (8), forms complete rows and columns
    cell_size: 2-5 (uniform within example)
    n_walls: 4-8 per axis, evenly spaced (spacing = cell_size + 1)
    wall_rows == wall_cols (symmetric lattice)
    last cell may be partial (size 1..cell_size-1) or walls may end at boundary [observed]
    total_cells_per_axis: 5-8 (including partial)
  background: black (0)
  markers:
    colors: exactly red (2) and green (3)
    each marker is a single pixel placed at a consistent offset within its cell (all markers in same example share identical offset)
    offset: approximately center of cell (floor for odd, varies for even)
    red: 2-4 marker cells forming a line or L-shape (1 h-run + 0-2 v-runs)
    green: 2 marker cells forming a single line (horizontal or vertical) [observed]
    every marker must participate in at least one same-color row or column pair
    markers never placed in the last partial cell row/col [observed]
    run span: 3-7 cells (38-88% of axis)
  output:
    fill entire lattice cells between endpoints of each same-color run (inclusive)
    preserve teal lattice lines
    cells covered by both a red and green run become pink (6)
    0-1 pink intersection cells per example [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick cell_size (2-5), n_walls (4-8). Compute grid_size. Optionally add partial cell.
    2. Build the lattice grid (teal rows/cols at spacing = cell_size + 1).
    3. Choose marker configurations for red and green on the coarse lattice:
       - Red: pick a line (2 cells same row or col) or L-shape (3 cells forming one h-run + one v-run, sharing a corner).
       - Green: pick a line (2 cells same row or col).
       - Ensure runs span >=3 cells. Optionally ensure one crossing for pink.
    4. Fill the output: color entire cells along each run, mark crossings as pink.
    5. Derive input by removing fills - keep only single marker pixels (at consistent cell offset) on a black+teal background.
```
